.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KEYS.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm keys.conf
.Nd keyboard shortcuts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/novi/keys.conf
.Pp
.Pa ~/.config/novi/keys.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every line is
.Ar action No = Ar binding ;
.Ql #
starts a comment.
Anything the file does not mention keeps the shortcut Novi ships
with, so changing one key means writing one line \(em not copying the
whole list out and keeping it in step forever.
.Pp
It is deliberately
.Em not
part of
.Xr system.conf 5 .
That file is the declared state of the machine, converged by
.Nm novi-state apply ;
nothing converges a keyboard shortcut.
.Nm novi-shell
reads these files once when it starts, which is also when a change
takes effect: log out and back in, or Alt+Escape and start the
desktop again.
.Ss Two files, and yours wins
.Pa /etc/novi/keys.conf
is the machine's and takes root to edit.
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/novi/keys.conf ,
or
.Pa ~/.config/novi/keys.conf ,
is applied after it, so yours is the one that fires.
.Pp
It layers
.Sy a line at a time ,
exactly as the machine's file layers over the shipped defaults: one
line in your file changes one shortcut and inherits every other.
Otherwise a machine-wide binding added later would never reach
anybody who had ever set one.
.Pp
.Nm novi-settings Ns 's
Keys panel writes
.Em your
file, never the machine's \(em a settings window quietly changing
every account is a thing to ask about rather than a default.
.Ss Writing a binding
.Bl -tag -width "Super+Return" -compact
.It Li Super+Return
Super, Win and Cmd all mean the same key.
.It Li Alt+Shift+T
Modifiers in any order.
.It Li Meta+Space
Meta means Alt.
.It Li Super+\&.
Punctuation as the character, not its name.
.It Li Print
A key with no modifier at all.
.It Li off
This shortcut does nothing.
.El
.Pp
Case does not matter:
.Li Super+Q
and
.Li super+q
are one binding, because the compositor compares lowercased and a
difference here would describe something that cannot happen.
.Pp
.Sy Control is not a modifier Novi binds .
A line naming Ctrl is
.Em refused ,
not half-applied \(em ignoring the word would bind the shortcut to
the bare letter, which does something far worse than what the line
says.
.Ss Actions
.Bl -tag -width "window.cycle-prev" -compact
.It Va window.cycle-next
.It Va window.cycle-prev
.It Va window.terminal
.It Va window.close
.It Va window.force-quit
Asks the program to close, exactly as
.Va window.close
does \(em and signals it instead when the desktop has already
established it cannot hear the request.
.It Va workspace.switch
.It Va workspace.move
Both take a digit range.
.It Va find.launcher
.It Va find.symbols
.It Va find.shortcuts
.It Va find.themes
.It Va find.notifications
.It Va find.screenshot
.It Va sound.up
.It Va sound.down
.It Va sound.mute
.It Va session.lock
.It Va session.stay-awake
.It Va session.power-menu
.It Va session.quit
.El
.Pp
The shipped
.Pa /etc/novi/keys.conf
names every one of them as a commented example, and is the only place
a person learns what an action is called.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.config/novi/keys.conf" -compact
.It Pa /etc/novi/keys.conf
The machine's.
Base content, not owned by any package: a file whose whole purpose is
to be edited must survive an upgrade.
.It Pa ~/.config/novi/keys.conf
Yours.
Created by the Keys panel if it does not exist.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Move the terminal onto Super+Shift+T and turn off the power menu:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
window.terminal    = Super+Shift+T
session.power-menu = off
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A line that cannot be parsed leaves
.Em its
row alone rather than failing the file: refusing nineteen working
shortcuts over one typo helps nobody.
An action name this build has never heard of is counted, not fatal
\(em one file is read by whatever
.Nm novi-shell
is installed.
.Pp
.Nm novi-launcher Fl -keys
(Super+/) shows what is
.Em in force ,
generated from the bindings that will actually fire, and says at the
top if a line could not be understood.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr novi-state 1 ,
.Xr system.conf 5
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0037.
.Sh CAVEATS
Two shortcuts cannot share a binding.
If a line moves one onto another's key, the one further down is left
.Sy unbound
and the sheet says so \(em better than silently never firing.
The check runs once after the last layer, so a clash your file
resolves is not counted.
.Pp
If this file locks you out, add
.Va novi.keys=off
to the kernel command line in the bootloader
.Pq press Ql e No on the GRUB entry
and Novi ignores
.Em both
files for that boot, exactly as
.Va novi.state=off
skips boot convergence.
The sheet ignores them too, so what you see is what you have.
